      "math_academy_id": "q-74588",
      "sequence_position": 14,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14224132/assets/cb0fa52f4f74145ee88e73f3f4c62d96d6b8261533844b4c8021d30cae8e41d0.png)\n\nWhich of the figures above is a rectangle?",
      "worked_solution": "First, recall the hierarchy diagram of quadrilaterals, where each shape below another is a specific type of the one above it.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14224132/assets/1dbe4c3e8ea97c0ac077c37143802366c5817d86965ab1dbc5abf54cd01d27c3.png)\n\nRemember that a rectangle is a quadrilateral where all angles are right and has two pairs of parallel sides.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each shape in turn.\n\n- The shape $P$ is an isosceles trapezoid. It has only one pair of parallel sides, not two, therefore, it is not a rectangle.\n\n- The shape $Q$ is a parallelogram. It has no right angles, therefore, it is not a rectangle.\n\n- The shape $R$ is a rhombus. Since it has no right angles, it is not a rectangle.\n\n- The shape $S$ is a rectangle since all its angles are right.\n\nTherefore, shape $S$ is a rectangle. The shapes $P,$ $Q,$ and $R$ are not rectangles.",
